    "response": "The short answer is **no, you should not make anything of the fact that the panel was entirely women, nor is it likely a sign of bias against you.** \n\nHere is a breakdown of why that is, along with what is more likely driving the outcome and the current silence.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Panel Composition\n* **No apparent gender bias:** Both you and the successful candidate are women. An all-female panel hiring a female candidate does not indicate gender discrimination.\n* **Why panels end up all-female:** In many universities, committee service is assigned based on rotation, availability, or institutional equity policies (which sometimes mandate minimum female representation, occasionally resulting in majority- or all-female panels depending on faculty availability). It can also simply reflect the leadership or composition of that specific division or teaching group.\n* **Making an issue of it would likely backfire:** Pointing to the panel's gender composition without any evidence of procedural irregularities would appear unfounded and could harm your professional reputation in what is often a small academic network.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why the Other Candidate May Have Been Selected\nAt the shortlist stage (the final three), hiring decisions rarely hinge purely on paper counts or who finished their degree first. Metrics get candidates onto the shortlist; the final decision usually comes down to strategic departmental fit and interview performance:\n\n* **Departmental Needs & Curriculum:** Economics departments frequently face pressure (from students, accreditation bodies, and university leadership) to offer applied, career-oriented courses (e.g., data analysis, financial markets, policy consulting). \"Strong industry experience\" often signals that the department specifically needed someone who could teach applied modules, bring real-world case studies, or connect students with industry internships.\n* **Fit vs. Metrics:** A candidate finishing a PhD with two papers who precisely fills a specific curriculum gap or research niche will often be preferred over a more credentialed candidate whose expertise duplicates existing faculty or doesn't align with current teaching vacancies.\n* **Job Talk and Teaching Demo:** Shortlist decisions are heavily swayed by how the committee perceived each candidate’s vision, teaching energy, student engagement, and collegiality during the interviews.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What About Age?\nAcademic hiring can occasionally harbor subtle biases toward candidates perceived as \"early career\" with long research runways. However:\n* A candidate with \"strong industry experience\" who is completing a PhD is frequently a mature or non-traditional student, not necessarily someone in their twenties.\n* Demonstrating age discrimination in academia is exceptionally difficult unless explicit, inappropriate statements were made during the process.\n\n---\n\n### 4. The Two-Week Delay in Feedback\n* **Standard university bureaucracy:** Two weeks is a very short time in academic administration. Search committees often cannot release formal feedback until the chosen candidate has formally signed the contract, HR has closed the requisition, and the committee chair's feedback notes have been vetted by faculty HR or the Dean's office.\n* Some institutions also have strict policies limiting or prohibiting detailed written feedback to unsuccessful candidates to avoid legal exposure.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Wait another week or two before following up:** Academic HR moves slowly. If you haven't heard back around the 3- to 4-week mark, a brief, professional follow-up is appropriate.\n2. **Keep the focus constructive:** Frame your follow-up around personal development rather than contesting the decision:\n   > *\"I understand you are very busy, but I wanted to gently follow up on my request for brief feedback from the recent interviews. As I continue on the job market, any guidance on how I can strengthen my profile for future lecturer vacancies in the department would be greatly appreciated.\"*\n3. **Focus on the positives:** Reaching the final three and going through two rounds of interviews proves your profile (PhD, six papers, four years of teaching) is competitive. The outcome in this instance was likely driven by a specific programmatic need (industry experience) rather than any deficiency on your part or bias from the panel.\n",
